{
 "id": "t_levels",
 "group": "thread",
 "num": 4,
 "title": "Atom to Organism",
 "subtitle": "Climb the levels of organization through Lectures 1 to 6",
 "lectures": "Lectures 1–6",
 "voice": "af_heart",
 "scenes": [
  {
   "lec": "L1 · Levels",
   "title": "The ladder",
   "img": "L1_01.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Chemical",
    "Cellular",
    "Tissue",
    "Organ",
    "Organism"
   ],
   "start": 0.4,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Lecture 1 gave you a ladder.",
     "start": 0.4,
     "end": 2.18
    },
    {
     "text": "Atom. Molecule. Macromolecule. Organelle.",
     "start": 2.63,
     "end": 5.57
    },
    {
     "text": "Cell. Tissue. Organ. Organ system. Organism.",
     "start": 6.02,
     "end": 9.1
    },
    {
     "text": "Lectures 2 through 6 climb it, one rung at a time.",
     "start": 9.55,
     "end": 12.58
    }
   ],
   "end": 13.68
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Atoms",
   "title": "Rung one: atoms",
   "img": "L2_01.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Protons",
    "Electrons",
    "Valence"
   ],
   "start": 13.68,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Atoms are the smallest unit of matter.",
     "start": 13.68,
     "end": 15.66
    },
    {
     "text": "**Protons** and neutrons sit in the nucleus. **Electrons** circle in shells.",
     "start": 16.11,
     "end": 20.67
    },
    {
     "text": "**Valence** electrons decide how atoms bond.",
     "start": 21.12,
     "end": 23.94
    },
    {
     "text": "Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen make up 96 percent of you.",
     "start": 24.39,
     "end": 29.25
    }
   ],
   "end": 30.35
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Bonds",
   "title": "Atoms join",
   "img": "L2_05.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Ionic",
    "Covalent",
    "Hydrogen bond"
   ],
   "start": 30.35,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Ionic** bonds form when one atom gives electrons to another.",
     "start": 30.35,
     "end": 34.11
    },
    {
     "text": "**Covalent** bonds share electrons. They're strong.",
     "start": 34.56,
     "end": 37.34
    },
    {
     "text": "**Hydrogen bonds** are weak links between molecules.",
     "start": 37.79,
     "end": 40.93
    },
    {
     "text": "They hold water together and hold DNA's two strands together.",
     "start": 41.38,
     "end": 44.98
    }
   ],
   "end": 46.08
  },
  {
   "lec": "L2 · Reactions",
   "title": "Building and breaking",
   "img": "L2_11.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Dehydration synthesis",
    "Hydrolysis"
   ],
   "start": 46.08,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Small molecules join into big ones by **dehydration synthesis**. Water comes out.",
     "start": 46.08,
     "end": 51.31
    },
    {
     "text": "Big molecules break into small ones by **hydrolysis**. Water goes in.",
     "start": 51.76,
     "end": 56.13
    },
    {
     "text": "That one pair of reactions builds every macromolecule.",
     "start": 56.58,
     "end": 59.99
    }
   ],
   "end": 61.09
  },
  {
   "lec": "L3 · Macromolecules",
   "title": "Rung three: the macros",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Carbohydrates",
    "Lipids",
    "Proteins",
    "Nucleic acids"
   ],
   "start": 61.09,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The four macromolecules are the macros on a nutrition label.",
     "start": 61.09,
     "end": 64.64
    },
    {
     "text": "**Carbohydrates** are fuel. Glucose, and glycogen for storage.",
     "start": 65.09,
     "end": 69.14
    },
    {
     "text": "**Lipids** store energy and build membranes.",
     "start": 69.59,
     "end": 72.02
    },
    {
     "text": "**Proteins** do most of the work: enzymes, transport, contraction, structure.",
     "start": 72.47,
     "end": 76.99
    },
    {
     "text": "**Nucleic acids**, DNA and RNA, hold the instructions.",
     "start": 77.44,
     "end": 81.29
    }
   ],
   "end": 82.39
  },
  {
   "lec": "L3 · Membrane",
   "title": "Lipids make a boundary",
   "img": "L3_10.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Phospholipid bilayer",
    "Cholesterol",
    "Membrane proteins"
   ],
   "start": 82.39,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Phospholipids line up tail to tail. That's the **phospholipid bilayer**.",
     "start": 82.39,
     "end": 86.73
    },
    {
     "text": "**Cholesterol** holds it together.",
     "start": 87.18,
     "end": 88.83
    },
    {
     "text": "**Membrane proteins** form channels and receptors.",
     "start": 89.28,
     "end": 92.07
    },
    {
     "text": "Now there's an inside and an outside. That's the start of a cell.",
     "start": 92.52,
     "end": 96.09
    }
   ],
   "end": 97.19
  },
  {
   "lec": "L3 · Transport",
   "title": "Crossing the boundary",
   "img": "L3_12.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Passive",
    "Active",
    "ATP"
   ],
   "start": 97.19,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "**Passive** transport needs no energy. Diffusion, osmosis, filtration.",
     "start": 97.19,
     "end": 101.99
    },
    {
     "text": "**Active** transport needs **ATP**.",
     "start": 102.44,
     "end": 104.45
    },
    {
     "text": "Endocytosis, exocytosis and the sodium potassium pump.",
     "start": 104.9,
     "end": 109.05
    }
   ],
   "end": 110.15
  },
  {
   "lec": "L4 · Organelles",
   "title": "Rung four: organelles",
   "img": "L4_01.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Nucleus",
    "Ribosomes",
    "ER",
    "Golgi",
    "Mitochondria"
   ],
   "start": 110.15,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Organelles are the cell's tiny organs.",
     "start": 110.15,
     "end": 112.33
    },
    {
     "text": "The **nucleus** holds the DNA.",
     "start": 112.78,
     "end": 114.52
    },
    {
     "text": "**Ribosomes** build proteins. The **ER** transports them. The **Golgi** packages them.",
     "start": 114.97,
     "end": 120.01
    },
    {
     "text": "**Mitochondria** make ATP.",
     "start": 120.46,
     "end": 122.28
    }
   ],
   "end": 123.38
  },
  {
   "lec": "L4 · DNA to protein",
   "title": "Instructions to action",
   "img": "L4_12.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Gene",
    "Transcription",
    "Translation"
   ],
   "start": 123.38,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "A **gene** is a stretch of DNA for one protein.",
     "start": 123.38,
     "end": 126.3
    },
    {
     "text": "**Transcription** copies it to messenger RNA in the nucleus.",
     "start": 126.75,
     "end": 130.61
    },
    {
     "text": "**Translation** builds the protein at a ribosome.",
     "start": 131.06,
     "end": 133.92
    },
    {
     "text": "So nucleic acids from Lecture 3 make the proteins from Lecture 3.",
     "start": 134.37,
     "end": 138.81
    }
   ],
   "end": 139.91
  },
  {
   "lec": "L4 · Cell cycle",
   "title": "Rung five: cells",
   "img": "L4_13.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Interphase",
    "Mitosis",
    "Cytokinesis"
   ],
   "start": 139.91,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The cell is the smallest unit of life.",
     "start": 139.91,
     "end": 142.01
    },
    {
     "text": "Cells grow during **interphase** and copy their DNA.",
     "start": 142.46,
     "end": 145.51
    },
    {
     "text": "**Mitosis** divides the nucleus. **Cytokinesis** splits the cell.",
     "start": 145.96,
     "end": 150.03
    },
    {
     "text": "That's how one cell becomes about 35 trillion.",
     "start": 150.48,
     "end": 153.3
    }
   ],
   "end": 154.4
  },
  {
   "lec": "L5 · Tissues",
   "title": "Rung six: tissues",
   "img": "L5_03.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Epithelial",
    "Connective",
    "Muscle",
    "Nervous"
   ],
   "start": 154.4,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Similar cells plus matrix make a tissue.",
     "start": 154.4,
     "end": 156.85
    },
    {
     "text": "**Epithelial** covers. **Connective** supports.",
     "start": 157.3,
     "end": 159.87
    },
    {
     "text": "**Muscle** moves. **Nervous** controls.",
     "start": 160.32,
     "end": 162.39
    },
    {
     "text": "Cell junctions hold them together: tight, gap and desmosome.",
     "start": 162.84,
     "end": 166.76
    }
   ],
   "end": 167.86
  },
  {
   "lec": "L6 · Skin",
   "title": "Rung seven: an organ",
   "img": "L6_05.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Epidermis",
    "Dermis",
    "Organ"
   ],
   "start": 167.86,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "An organ is two or more tissue types.",
     "start": 167.86,
     "end": 170.13
    },
    {
     "text": "Skin is the largest **organ**.",
     "start": 170.58,
     "end": 172.33
    },
    {
     "text": "Its **epidermis** is epithelium. Its **dermis** is connective tissue.",
     "start": 172.78,
     "end": 176.82
    },
    {
     "text": "It has smooth muscle in the arrector pili, and nerve endings for touch.",
     "start": 177.27,
     "end": 181.44
    },
    {
     "text": "All four tissues, in one organ.",
     "start": 181.89,
     "end": 183.8
    }
   ],
   "end": 184.9
  },
  {
   "lec": "L6 · System",
   "title": "Rung eight: a system",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Integumentary system",
    "Hair",
    "Nails",
    "Glands"
   ],
   "start": 184.9,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Skin plus **hair**, **nails** and **glands** makes the **integumentary system**.",
     "start": 184.9,
     "end": 188.92
    },
    {
     "text": "It's one of eleven organ systems.",
     "start": 189.37,
     "end": 191.21
    },
    {
     "text": "All eleven together make the organism. You.",
     "start": 191.66,
     "end": 194.44
    }
   ],
   "end": 195.54
  },
  {
   "lec": "Recap",
   "title": "Climb it again",
   "img": null,
   "terms": [
    "Atom",
    "Molecule",
    "Cell",
    "Tissue",
    "Organ"
   ],
   "start": 195.54,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Atoms bond into molecules.",
     "start": 195.54,
     "end": 197.32
    },
    {
     "text": "Molecules build membranes and organelles.",
     "start": 197.77,
     "end": 200.21
    },
    {
     "text": "Organelles run the cell. Cells divide into many.",
     "start": 200.66,
     "end": 203.69
    },
    {
     "text": "Cells form tissues. Tissues form organs like skin.",
     "start": 204.14,
     "end": 207.41
    },
    {
     "text": "Each level builds on the one before. Structure determines function.",
     "start": 207.86,
     "end": 211.78
    }
   ],
   "end": 212.88
  }
 ],
 "duration": 212.88,
 "audio": "stories/t_levels.mp3"
}